C LB+FADD
"CSRA allows, Promising (1.0) forbids -- the relaxed-RMW load buffering of
 'Bridging the Gap' Example 3.10. P0 reads y into a and writes z=a; P1 reads z
 into b, does c=fetch_add(x,1), and writes y=c+1. Target a=1 /\ b=1 /\ c=0.
 CSRA ALLOWS it: its POWER-like, non-multi-copy-atomic storage subsystem admits
 the whole load-buffering family (CSRA supports RMWs; see CSRA section 8).
 Promising 1.0 FORBIDS it: realising y=1 needs a promise of y=1 that fails
 certification under future-memory quantification -- the same uncertifiable-RMW
 mechanism documented as the PS-1.0 -> ARMv8 compilation unsoundness. (Promising
 2.0 repairs the RMW case, narrowing this separation.) Neither model is in
 herd7; verdicts CITED. This file documents the shape. See README."
{ int x=0; int y=0; int z=0; }
P0 (atomic_int* x, atomic_int* y, atomic_int* z) {
  int a = atomic_load_explicit(y,memory_order_relaxed);
  atomic_store_explicit(z,a,memory_order_relaxed);
}
P1 (atomic_int* x, atomic_int* y, atomic_int* z) {
  int b = atomic_load_explicit(z,memory_order_relaxed);
  int c = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(x,1,memory_order_relaxed);
  atomic_store_explicit(y,c+1,memory_order_relaxed);
}
exists (0:a=1 /\ 1:b=1 /\ 1:c=0)
